# Belt and braces. The dockerfile copies an explicit allowlist, so nothing here
# should ever reach the image anyway — this exists so that if someone later
# reverts to `COPY . .`, the repository does not become the webroot.

.git
.git/**
.gitignore
.github

# Server and build configuration. Publishing these tells an attacker exactly
# which headers are set, where certificates are expected, and what is not covered.
dockerfile
Dockerfile
.dockerignore
# .htaccess is deliberately NOT ignored: it is a published file, copied into
# the webroot by the dockerfile so the local container runs the real config.

# Internal documentation. Written for us, not for visitors.
*.md
docs
AGENTS.md
README.md

# Editor and OS noise
.vscode
.idea
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
*.swp
*~

# Anything that smells like a credential. None of these are in the repo today;
# the pattern is here so an accident does not become a publication.
.env
.env.*
*.pem
*.key
*.crt
*.p12
*.pfx
id_rsa*
*.tfvars
secrets*
